pping Input Shapes to Output Cavities**\n\nI'm finding that the grey shapes from the input are indeed being mapped into the empty spaces, or cavities, of the output grid. It appears to be a translation process, with shapes fitting snugly into these defined areas.\n\n**Identifying Shape Placement Logic**\n\nI'm realizing the grey shapes are not just coloring empty space blue, but are instead acting as puzzle pieces that fit into specific cavities. The key seems to be translation without rotation, filling these gaps precisely.\n\n**Revisiting Shape-Cavity Mapping**\n\nI'm re-evaluating the mapping of grey shapes to blue outputs. It appears the shapes are not simply becoming blue, but are fitting into cavities within the red structure. The internal '1's might represent these cavities.\n\n**Analyzing Grid Components**\n\nI'm currently focused on the red and empty zones at the top of the grid, and the grey shapes in the lower section. I'm trying to understand how these grey elements are transformed into the blue output.\n\n**Verifying Cavity-Shape Fit**\n\nI'm confirming that the tops of the grey shapes precisely align with the ceilings of the cavities. Shape A matches a flat, three-unit wide ceiling, and Shape C fits a single-unit wide ceiling, solidifying the lock-and-key idea.\n\n**Investigating Nuanced Alignment**\n\nI'm noticing that the alignment isn't always a direct match of external profiles. A specific segment of a shape in the input doesn't directly translate to a '1' in the output, suggesting a more complex matching logic than simple alignment.\n\n**Confirming Shape-Cavity Translation**\n\nI'm confirming that shapes are translated into cavities without rotation. My current focus is on the exact logic of how they \"touch\" the red ceiling to ensure a perfect, constrained fit.\n\n**Exploring Cavity Definition Precision**\n\nI'm re-examining the test input to precisely define the cavities. I'm identifying distinct empty regions within the red area and mapping th